WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.150
Risk combines likelihood, impact, and context.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.940
A highly likely event with low impact may be manageable.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.840
A low-probability event with catastrophic impact may still deserve serious attention.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.380
Context decides how much risk matters.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.100
The same technical failure might be tolerable in a prototype and unacceptable in a

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.720
production safety system.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.885
Expected value is a high-level way to think about outcomes by combining likelihood and

00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:22.620
consequence.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.700
If an option has a modest chance of a large benefit and a small downside,

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.855
it may be attractive.

00:00:26.855 --> 00:00:30.615
If an option has a high chance of a small benefit and a meaningful chance of severe

00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:31.040
damage,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.155
it may not be.

00:00:32.155 --> 00:00:33.905
Expected value is not a magic formula,

00:00:33.905 --> 00:00:38.055
but it helps people avoid focusing only on the most emotionally vivid outcome.

00:00:38.055 --> 00:00:39.656
Average expectations are not enough.

00:00:39.656 --> 00:00:42.795
Tail risks are rare but severe outcomes that can dominate the decision.

00:00:42.795 --> 00:00:47.390
A plan may look fine on average but still expose the organization to a worst credible case

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:48.520
it cannot absorb.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.935
Security, operations, safety,

00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:53.190
and continuity planning all require attention to what could happen at the edge,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.541
not just what is most likely.

00:00:54.541 --> 00:00:57.320
Fragile decisions fail sharply when conditions change.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.150
Resilient decisions preserve options, limit blast radius, include monitoring,

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.520
and make recovery possible.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.265
Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact.

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:08.025
Contingency planning prepares action if the risk materializes.

00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:12.070
Risk tolerance defines how much uncertainty and downside the decision maker is willing and

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:12.800
able to accept.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.960
A responsible decision process considers upside, downside, and survivability.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.540
It asks what success looks like, what failure looks like,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.910
what signals would show the situation is changing,

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.375
and what action would be taken if assumptions break.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:26.295
Good risk thinking is not fear-based.

00:01:26.295 --> 00:01:29.140
It is a practical way to keep action aligned with consequences.
